<!--META-DATA
{
	tags: for, indexing,
}
-->

<statement>
<p>
	Given an (x,y) coordinate on the tic-tac-toe interface, this program returns the absolute index corresponding to the game state data matrix.
</p>

<tabular valign="top" top="minor">
	<col width="12%"/>
	<col width="88%"/>
	<row bottom="minor">
		<cell>Inputs:</cell>
		<cell>
			<p><pf>x</pf> (1 x 1) integer — positive integer. The x-coordinate of the bottom left corner of a space.</p>
			<p><pf>y</pf> (1 x 1) integer — positive integer. The y-coordinate of the bottom left corner of a space.</p>
		</cell>
	</row>
	<row bottom="minor">
		<cell>Outputs:</cell>
		<cell>
			<p><pf>abs_index</pf> (1 x 1) integer — positive integer. The absolute index of the corresponding value on <pf>game_state.data</pf>.</p>
		</cell>
	</row>
	<row>
		<cell>Details:</cell>
		<cell><p>You can hardcode this program specifically for the tic-tac-toe game. That is,</p></cell>
	</row>
	<row>
		<cell/>
		<cell><p>the only (x,y) values that your code needs to be able to handle will be the 9 bottom left corner points of each space as shown below</p></cell>
	</row>
	<row>
		<cell/>
		<cell><p>.</p></cell>
	</row>
	<row bottom="minor">
		<cell/>
		<cell><p>You may code this however you like, but try to come up with a better way than using 9 if-else branches.</p></cell>
	</row>
</tabular>

<p>
	Examples:
<program>
	<code>
		click_2_index(3, 2)
		8
		click_2_index(1, 1)
		3
	</code>
</program>
</p>
</statement>
